WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:20.789
I think Allison touched on a lot of
points as to why it would be beneficial

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:26.220
for graduate students and people on
certain career trajectories to think

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:31.650
about science outreach and education and
science communication but I also want to

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:37.559
point out that doing this as an
institution is not charity. There is an

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:43.739
amazing return on investment if an
institution were to invest in science

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:49.460
outreach and I'm living, breathing proof
of that.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:56.550
So when I think about my story I really
want to tell it in a way that's useful

00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:00.239
and so I was really Disan and I were
walking down the street to the story

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:06.119
Collider last night and I was sort of
throwing all of my ideas at her and she

00:01:06.119 --> 00:01:10.920
sort of helped me find clarity and a
single word to describe what I think

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.490
really describes my experience in
science outreach and that's reciprocity.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.959
So here's how my story started: So I was
a postdoc here. I spent a lot of time in

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.700
the lab and I also spent a lot of time
doing scicomm stuff, doing outreach stuff. I

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.170
volunteered for the bio bus, Lou and I
and another couple of colleagues we

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.020
would do this discussion series called
spot on NYC. It was science on NYC

00:01:35.039 --> 00:01:38.009
and then they were like oh you
can't use that word so we went to spot

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:43.410
on NYC and we were just engaging with
communities in ways that brought people

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:50.670
together on this campus in really unique
and creative ways. One of the

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:55.979
beauties of Rockefeller is that they
kind of like let you do what you want to

00:01:55.979 --> 00:01:59.459
do as long as you're not interfering
with anybody else's dream of doing what

00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:04.979
they want to do so it's pretty great. So I knew I wanted to continue my career

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:08.129
inside common outreach because those
experiences were really transformative

00:02:08.129 --> 00:02:11.970
for me and I also knew I wanted to stay
at Rockefeller so I made a hail-mary

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.840
pitch to Marc Tessier-Lavigne who was the
then president and I laid out all of

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.470
these initiatives around science science
communication and outreach and I said

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:22.860
and I would like to be the person that
does this,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.180
wink wink, right? And he was like oh
sounds good we have an open position

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.060
right now for
science outreach program you should

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:35.599
apply for it. I was like oh my god what
timing. It was amazing!

00:02:37.349 --> 00:02:43.890
So I within like three days I was now
the director of science outreach at

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.780
Rockefeller. I was like oh my god what do
I do with this. But I wasn't the

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.050
first person. So we actually had an
outreach program it was started in 1992

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.040
by this awesome lady
Bonnie Kaiser, many of the West Coasters

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.460
maybe know her. Right so and she ran
something called a summer science

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.310
research program for high school
students. This is where we

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:07.620
take a small portion of high school
students that are top of their class and

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.400
bring them in for seven weeks and they
do some sort of research with one of the

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.450
labs here all over Rockefeller and I
thought that was a really great start

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:19.349
but I knew that those kids that were
already science interested they were

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:23.849
already being coached by parents that
were really invested in their time and

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:28.590
invested in their futures where maybe
not the kids that could really use the

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:33.510
science outreach. I knew that from my own
experience how hard it was for me to

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:39.120
sort of break out of my culture in my
sort of home life where my parents are

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.370
you know I'm a product of a teenage
pregnancy. My parents you know they have

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.180
a salary job or an hourly job and you
know they're like why don't you just go

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.980
to work. This is what you do, you graduate
college, you go to work, right? They didn't

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:57.630
understand this whole thing so I knew
the uphill battle and so I wanted to

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:01.709
help more kids like that. So I knew
the first thing I had to do was beg for

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:06.810
space. So I went to the person who's in
charge of space here at Rockefeller and

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:10.560
every day I went to his office for six
months and I said I would like some

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.650
space now. And he's like okay I'll think
about it. I would like to some space now.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:17.400
Okay I'll think about it. And finally he
was like fine. Okay so first lesson is be

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:23.580
super annoying because squeaky squeaky
wheels get the oil right? But what I

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:29.400
haven't mentioned yet is the vision of
the development office. Okay so the

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:35.130
development office for me was absolutely
key. There's a woman who runs the

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:39.300
development office her name is Marni
Inhofe. She is basically she doesn't know

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.390
this but like I bow to her. She is my
mentor.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:46.290
She is the smartest person I've ever met
in my life and she sees the potential

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:53.370
that no one else sees and with her
support it really give gave me a path to

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:59.970
building RockEDU and so once I got
this space Marni sees this potential and

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:04.530
then allows her team to start helping me
in coaching me better understand the

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:09.420
institutional infrastructure. I came in
as a scientist. I had the backing of the

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.980
science community already. I got I know
the lingo, I've been in meetings, you know

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:21.260
I sort of have that support but what I
didn't understand is how to integrate a

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:25.440
program into the infrastructure and the
Development Office provided me that

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.190
clarity. They have been mentoring me for
many many years and without them I

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:34.710
wouldn't be here
and so Marni helped me get a my first

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:39.030
bit of funding from a foundation and
with that I was able to hire a full-time

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:43.500
administrator and another scientist to
help run a program called Lab

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.400
Experiences. She even coined this thing
called LAB initiative: learning at the

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.470
bench and I like she's a genius. I just
love her. So we have this thing called

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.740
lab initiative and it allowed me to
start building up some hands-on

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.540
programming to recruit people from the
communities that I really wanted to

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:10.380
serve and so I started working with
teachers and I started asking them to

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:14.130
bring their kids into the lab and doing
some stuff and while and we did what we

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.760
knew right and as the teachers would
come in we would host these classrooms

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.350
of students. We would stop the teachers
and say tell us what we did wrong. Tell

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.950
us how we can do better. What do you
actually need? And over the course of

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:29.190
time we started building up this program
and iterating failing and iterating and

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:33.000
failing and iterating until we're sort
of getting it right now I think.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.840
Odaelys gets much more credit than I
do. She runs the lab experiences. So she

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:46.260
so Marni sees this and we are deciding
that you know what this is a really

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:50.370
successful situation let's do more. Right
so we started thinking about ways that

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:55.020
we can synergize and we know that
science outreach

00:06:55.020 --> 00:07:00.539
is something that donors really
can connect with. So I started

00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:05.190
doing these things with the development
office where I would attend some of

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:09.539
these really high-end development events
and talk to the people there and sort of

00:07:09.539 --> 00:07:14.669
get them interested in the way we are
approaching science outreach at

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:18.659
Rockefeller and that allowed them to
sort of have a better insight into how

00:07:18.659 --> 00:07:23.610
they or if they might want to add
support to the science here. And so

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:28.639
we started doing this thing called
Science Saturday, which is a festival for

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:34.440
families around the the New York area.
You know it's just a one-day thing but

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:39.870
it's a really big endeavor and it allows
us to recruit new donors to the campus.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:43.680
They see the campus. They see the
scientists. We have about 35 to 40

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.900
different science stations run by actual
scientists. And by the way what happened

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:54.270
with Science Saturday is that it created
a sense of community on this campus that

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:59.580
was never really fully realized prior. So
people are coming out of like the labs

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:03.659
the woodwork of the labs to do this
thing and they're saying to themselves

00:08:03.659 --> 00:08:08.490
I've never interacted with my colleagues
the way I interact with them you know

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:11.789
when I interact with them in a lecture
setting it's really you know this very

00:08:11.789 --> 00:08:16.949
dry situation but what I'm doing this as
part of science outreach what I'm doing

00:08:16.949 --> 00:08:21.449
feels so fulfilling. It feels so great, I
feel so inspired and I feel so energized

00:08:21.449 --> 00:08:26.190
and it creates this happiness which i
think is really important so the

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:31.380
reciprocity is there for the scientists
in giving them that that happiness that

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.010
they might be craving even though
they're like killing it on nature papers

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:39.089
and science papers and faculty positions.
This place is intense with science but

00:08:39.089 --> 00:08:42.360
there's still that sort of emptiness and
science outreach really helps them to

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:48.360
fulfill it and so so we started really
building out more and more and more. So

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:51.720
working with a development team we're
you know we're using science outreach to

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.500
bring in donors. We're using science
outreach to train graduate students on

00:08:55.500 --> 00:09:00.270
how to teach, how to mentor, how do we
have this whole thing on mentoring we we

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:04.620
train graduate students on science
communication skills. We're helping them

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.600
get jobs, right. So this is the reciprocity. They come and they volunteer and

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:14.270
they do stuff for us and then we in turn
help them along their career

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:20.720
trajectories. We are helping faculty. They now see the value that we can give

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:27.770
them in terms of you know how they can
approach their science. We are allowing

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:32.480
them to write really rigorous broader
impact statements. We are allowing them

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:38.180
to contribute to other types of
community engagement and outreach where

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:42.740
they're actually fulfilling something.
Not just doing it out of the goodness of

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:47.390
their hearts but they're meeting some
criteria that they had to meet that they

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:52.550
didn't necessarily have a mechanism for.
I also want to point out that the

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:56.899
reciprocity doesn't just stop with you
know working with development. It doesn't

00:09:56.899 --> 00:10:01.100
just stop with graduate students,
postdocs, and faculty. It also the

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:05.060
reciprocity is also really important
here with our support staff. I don't know

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:08.360
if you've noticed me like high-fiving
our custodians. I don't know if you've

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:12.740
noticed me hugging you know the people
doing our catering. It's because they see

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:17.270
also science outreach as a mechanism for
their families to be involved and I am

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:21.860
always inviting them to participate and
supporting the students or their

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:26.720
children or their families in some way
and because of that and because we are

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:30.980
an organization that sort of operates
with a really small budget and kind of

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:35.180
under the radar of a lot of things I
need a lot of favors to make this stuff

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:40.520
what it is, right. And so when I do these
things for the custodial staff or the

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:44.510
catering staff or the teams that are
working behind the scenes and I'm like

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:50.480
oh my god I need like XYZ setup like
tomorrow I'm sorry you know they're like

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:54.980
we got you right and that helps us run
really smoothly too. So I think it's

00:10:54.980 --> 00:11:01.160
really important to recognize that and
so lastly one of the other ROIs

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:05.450
that I think the institution has
experienced through the work that we're

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:09.829
doing in science outreach is this sort
of community relationship with with the

00:11:09.829 --> 00:11:15.470
New York City like government and and
policy positions. So I don't know if

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:20.230
you've noticed but we have this new
building being built which is like

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:26.170
a segment of 19 giant things that got
sort of it's like a twenty

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:30.880
floor skyscraper but instead of being
like this it's like on top of the FDR. It

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:34.510
was actually really incredible. They like
lifted it from a barge in nineteen seconds

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:39.250
and like dropped it on top of the FDR
on these like little lego segment. But

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:43.570
in order to get the permit for that, that
is a capital improvement for this

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:47.560
university, in order to get the permit
for that they had to prove to the New

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:51.220
York City offices that they did
community engagement and that they were

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:56.290
serving the community and guess who
provided the numbers for them. Me, right.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:12:00.670
So this is a really important aspect
that we're not necessarily always

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.630
talking about. We always want to frame
science outreach as what how we can help

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:08.529
the community, how we can help graduate
students, how we can help postdocs. We're

00:12:08.529 --> 00:12:12.399
never really thinking about it, at least
in my conversations with others, we're

00:12:12.399 --> 00:12:17.110
not always thinking about it in terms of
like how can we leverage this to help

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:20.740
the university or the institution that
you're working in. Right and so I just

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:25.660
wanted to point out that the ROIs for
the university are many and can be

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:29.890
leveraged in really smart ways and I
think we need to add these to the

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:35.410
conversations. I'm gonna just end in one
second and say that my amazing

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:38.949
